{
  "example": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.NodeData`\n\nAn application that reviews requirements and source code can distinguish both\nkinds of node with one enum:\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]\npub enum ReviewNodeData {\n    Requirement(Vec<String>),\n    Source {\n        file: String,\n        selector: String,\n    },\n}\n```\n\nIts `Domain` implementation sets `type NodeData = ReviewNodeData;`. For example,\n`ReviewNodeData::Requirement(vec![\"sdk\".into(), \"errors\".into()])` identifies a\nrequirement, while `ReviewNodeData::Source { file: \"src/lib.rs\".into(),\nselector: \"fn[validate]\".into() }` identifies a function.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The example `requirements.sdk.graph.node-kinds` is a concrete case. A single enum has `Requirement(Vec<String>)` and `Source { file, selector }` variants. The domain sets its `NodeData` to that enum. A requirement value and a source value each identify a different kind of node. The test `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[enum_node_ids_distinguish_requirements_and_source_items_in_one_domain]` has a `{test}` reference to it.\n\nThe test declares a local `ReviewNodeId` enum with the same derives and variants as the example (`Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash`). Its `ReviewDomain` sets `type NodeData = ReviewNodeId;`. It uses the example's exact values: `Requirement([\"sdk\",\"errors\"])` and `Source{file:\"src/lib.rs\", selector:\"fn[validate]\"}`.\n\nThe assertions check each outcome:\n- `assert_ne!(requirement, source)` checks that the two kinds are distinct values.\n- The `node_path` assertions check that the requirement interns to `[\"requirement\",\"sdk\",\"errors\"]` and the source to `[\"source\",\"src/lib.rs\",\"fn[validate]\"]`. A three-segment requirement is checked too.\n- The `outgoing_edges` assertions check that the requirement node leads to the interned source node and that the source node has no edges. Re-interning equal data gives the same node.\n- The `read(...).format()` assertions check that each kind resolves to its own content.\n- The dependency `matches!` assertion checks that the requirement and source are recorded as separate dependencies.\n- The `store_evidence` and `load_evidence` assertions check that evidence stored for the requirement loads back for it and is absent for the source node.\n\nThese assertions use values derived from the example. The cited `requirements.sdk.graph.NodeData` rule is that equal data identifies the same node and different data identifies a different node. The test applies it, because re-interned equal values resolve to the same node and the two kinds stay separate. I found no outcome in the example that the test leaves unasserted.",
